<article class="article-shell">
<header class="article-header"><p class="article-kicker">DNS · 8 MIN READ · AUGUST 28, 2026</p><h1>DNS Delegation Explained</h1><p class="article-dek">From the root zone to the nameserver that owns an answer: a visual tour of delegation, glue, recursion, and DNSSEC.</p><div class="article-header-meta"><span>StackHal Field Notes</span><span>Infrastructure guide</span></div></header>
<div class="article-layout"><aside class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><span>ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#chain">The chain</a><a href="#delegation">Delegation</a><a href="#glue">Glue</a><a href="#resolvers">Resolvers</a><a href="#dnssec">DNSSEC</a><a href="#checklist">Trace checklist</a></aside>
<div class="article-content">
<p id="chain" class="article-lead">When a recursive resolver looks up <code>www.example.com</code>, it does not ask one global database for the answer. It follows referrals down a hierarchy until an authoritative nameserver can answer for the zone that contains the name.</p>
<figure class="article-visual dns-chain"><div class="dns-node"><b>ROOT</b><span>.</span><small>refers to .com</small></div><i>↓</i><div class="dns-node"><b>TLD</b><span>.com</span><small>refers to example.com</small></div><i>↓</i><div class="dns-node"><b>AUTHORITATIVE</b><span>ns1.example.com</span><small>answers www.example.com</small></div><i>↓</i><div class="dns-node dns-node-result"><b>ANSWER</b><span>203.0.113.42</span><small>A record</small></div><figcaption>A delegation is a referral: each parent points the resolver toward the child zone.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="article-callout article-callout-accent"><strong>Delegation is not the same as resolution</strong><span>The parent zone publishes who is responsible for a child zone. The authoritative server for that child publishes the actual A, AAAA, MX, TXT, and other records.</span></div>
<h2 id="delegation">1. Root, TLD, and the delegation cut</h2><p>The root zone is the top of the DNS hierarchy. Root nameservers do not store every website’s address; they know which nameservers serve each top-level domain such as <code>.com</code>, <code>.org</code>, or a country-code TLD.</p><p>The TLD nameserver then returns the delegation for <code>example.com</code>: a set of NS names, and sometimes glue addresses. The child zone begins at that delegation cut. Its authoritative nameservers own the records below it.</p>
<pre class="article-code"><code>example.com.  172800  IN  NS  ns1.dns-host.example
example.com.  172800  IN  NS  ns2.dns-host.example
www.example.com. 300 IN A 203.0.113.42</code></pre>
<h2 id="glue">2. NS records and glue are different evidence</h2><p>An NS record names a server. Glue is an address supplied by the parent when the nameserver’s hostname lives inside the child zone. Without glue, a resolver could need the child zone’s address before it can reach the child zone—a circular dependency.</p>
<div class="article-screen screen-dns" role="img" aria-label="Illustration of StackHal DNS DAG Tracer showing root, TLD, delegation, and authoritative nodes"><div class="screen-chrome"><span class="screen-dot"></span><span class="screen-dot"></span><span class="screen-dot"></span><span>stackhal.com / dns-dag-tracer</span></div><div class="dns-screen-body"><div class="dns-toolbar"><strong>DNS DAG TRACER</strong><span class="screen-status">CHAIN COMPLETE</span></div><div class="dns-mini-chain"><span>root</span><b>→</b><span>.com</span><b>→</b><span>example.com NS</span><b>→</b><span>ns1 authoritative</span></div><div class="dns-evidence"><span>delegation found</span><span>glue required · present</span><span>DNSSEC · validated</span></div></div><span class="screen-caption">Trace the referrals and see where an answer becomes authoritative.</span></div>
<h2 id="resolvers">3. Recursive resolvers do the walking</h2><p>Your browser usually talks to a stub resolver, which asks a recursive resolver operated by an ISP, company, or public DNS service. The recursive resolver performs the root-to-authority walk, caches referrals and answers according to TTL, and returns the result to the client.</p><p>A recursive resolver is not authoritative merely because it returns an answer. It may be answering from cache. To inspect ownership, ask for the authority section or query the authoritative server directly.</p>
<h2 id="dnssec">4. DNSSEC authenticates the chain</h2><p>DNSSEC adds signatures to DNS data. A validating resolver checks a signed record with its DNSKEY, follows DS records from the parent, and builds a chain of trust back to a trusted root key. A valid signature supports authenticity and integrity; it does not encrypt DNS traffic.</p><p>If the chain is broken, a validating resolver can return <code>SERVFAIL</code> instead of an answer. That is different from <code>NXDOMAIN</code>, which means the authoritative system says the name does not exist.</p>
<h2 id="checklist">A practical delegation trace</h2><ol class="article-checklist"><li><strong>Ask the root:</strong> identify the TLD referral.</li><li><strong>Ask the TLD:</strong> record the child zone’s NS set and glue.</li><li><strong>Ask the authority:</strong> query the exact record type at the delegated server.</li><li><strong>Compare views:</strong> separate cached recursive answers from authoritative answers.</li><li><strong>Check TTLs:</strong> confirm whether an old answer is still within cache lifetime.</li><li><strong>Validate DNSSEC:</strong> inspect DS, DNSKEY, RRSIG, and the final validation state.</li></ol>
<div class="article-cta"><p class="article-kicker">READY TO TRACE A DOMAIN?</p><h2>See the DNS graph, hop by hop.</h2><p>DNS DAG Tracer turns referrals, nameserver ownership, glue, and DNSSEC evidence into a readable path.</p><div class="article-cta-actions"><a class="article-button article-button-primary" href="{{ path('dns_dag_tracer') }}">Open DNS DAG Tracer →</a><a class="article-button" href="{{ path('blog_index') }}">Back to the blog</a></div></div>
